Date: Fri, 10 Sep 93 11:48:35 EDT
From: eisrael@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
Subject: BOOK: The Selfish Gene

Phil G. Fraering writes:
> My first comment: other than for purposes of general self-improvement,
> what is this book on the reading list for? I can see where discussion
> of evolution would be one reason, but noone's recommended Gould so
> far... or other authors...
> 
> I read the chapter on memes in the bookstore a couple years back.
> Was that basically _it_?

Well, that's one thing, but it isn't everything I think can be found in
that book. I think the ideas concerning selfishness and altruism and his
use of game theory dovetail nicely into discussions of social systems
(although Dawkins himself goes to some effort to distance himself from
the idea).

I found that I was able to integrate the ideas into a larger space, having
to do with self-organizing systems like markets as well as species. The
book gets you thinking along more extropian lines, and not just with memes
alone. (In fact, the memes meme, while interesting, is probably the least
interesting thing I learned from that book. I'd rate the integration of
the ethography with economics somewhat higher.)

It underscores the important and frequently ignored idea that cooperation
and competition are two sides of the same coin, a concept that applies equally
well to discussions of markets and of allelles.

For the objectivists on the list, try comparing and contrasting Rand's view
of selfishness with Dawkins finding that true altruism is virtually absent
in the animal kingdom.

For fans of Camille Paglia, try comparing her views on gender roles, history,
and art with Dawkins' chapter on reproductive strategies.

While reading this book, I got a sense that I was seeing a major piece of
the puzzle. I should note, however, that not everyone finds as much value
in that book as I did. (Rowan should be encouraged to give his view; his
father had some expertise in this area.)

