Date: Fri, 10 Sep 1993 11:49:36 -0400
From: "Perry E. Metzger" <pmetzger@lehman.com>
Subject: The Discoveries of Concepts and Rights 

starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu says:
> Not only are concepts most certainly not any kind of invention whatsoever,
> neither are rights.  To dispose of the claim that concepts are made, rather
> than found, requires nothing more than to consider the question: how can
> anyone invent anything without already having any concepts?  They can't.

Sounds very much like the notion that evolution can't occur, Tim. You
can invent things without concepts -- but until you invent concepts
you can't conceptualize what you've invented. Nature, unconsciously,
does lots of inventing -- look at the invention of photosynthesis, for
example -- a great invention, but certainly not one requiring the
existance of consciousness, let alone concepts.

> This is the fallacy of self-exclusion, also known as the problem of self-
> referential refutation.  (Most of the worst fallacies of today are of this
> kind.)

No, Tim. You're the one suffering from a fallacy here -- the fallacy
that what you are doing is formal reasoning, when in fact you can't do
formal reasoning on anything other than a formal system. If you use
english words, you are no longer being formal, because english is
slippery. Unless you can express the things you are reasoning about in
some sort of formal language -- the sort that even logicians rarely
resort to -- you can't be absolutely certain that you can do deductive
reasoning. You can use deductive reasoning to approximately model what
you are doing, but you can't rely on it. Thats the problem with the
Ayn Rand model of the universe -- it claims that you can know what the
axioms are and that you can do reasoning in english on slippery and
poorly defined concepts. This leads to conclusions like the idea that
the evidence of your senses must be correct (we have proof otherwise
and soon will be able to construct virtual realities for which this
will truly not hold at all), or the idea that smoking is obviously a
good idea and not smoking is an irrational denial of gratification.

> The fundamental error in the discussion of rights is the conceptual
> dualism stemming from Aristotle (in which case he actually did make
> a mistake, unlike his metaphysics) that all things are either made
> by man, or not a result of human action at all.  This omits
> spontaneous orders altogether.  (It continues to astound me how so
> many who post to this list fail to appreciate fully the significance
> and implications of this concept!)

This is philosophical double talk, Tim. In concrete terms, what you
claim is that there are a set of natural rights that can be
established by pure reason. However, you cannot present me with any
evidence of this contention -- in particular, your "calculus of human
rights" doesn't exist. Presumably, were rights truly derivable from
pure reason, I could state a clear set of axioms that would give me a
consistant definition of rights no matter what the situation -- and
yet, no such set of axioms has ever been presented.

> Primarily, rights are not the result of human design, just like
> money, language, and many other social formations.  They result from
> human action, but not from human design.

Really Tim? In 1st Century Rome, your parents own you and can decide
to perform a retroactive abortion in your 30th year. In 20th Century
America, your parents don't even have the right to embarass you in
public. So tell me -- which are the "true" set of human rights?  There
is no objective way to tell. Its all a matter of what people choose.

No rights are "natural". Some sets of rights are the sort that I'd
like to see, and some sets are the sort that I wouldn't like to see.
Claiming that they are inherent is innane -- it not only goes against
evidence, but it even goes against reasoning, which you seem to think
is more primary. If rights are inherent and absolute, I should be able
to figure out what the "true" rights someone has are in any situation
-- and yet there are many situations like the question of childrens
rights in which the issues are too stick to resolve based on the
simple rules we've selected.

What you have, Tim, is not a set of axioms and theorems. What you have
is a model. Most scientists know that when you find that your model
has a hole in it -- your physics model fails to accurately predict the
orbit of Mercury, say -- then you know that your model isn't good
enough for hard cases and needs to be replaced with a more complete
model.

Now, I can model human rights pretty well by deriving them from
decisions made in the context of human interactions. Rights tend to be
legal schelling points selected by people in the course of daily
interaction and later reified by custom. However, the notion that they
are not the mere result of custom and actually have some "inherent
reality" about them doesn't seem to be borne out by observation.

> The notion that these things are made, rather than found, is just as
> creationist as the notion that the Universe and everything in it are the
> Creation of some Supreme Being.  There is no such Supreme Being, Divine
> or secular.

And yet, humans build computers, and construct legal systems. Contract
law as we know it is very much a human creation -- just as much a
human creation as word processors.

I find it interestingly inconsistant, by the way, that on the one hand
you rail against the notion of a creator, and yet claim that there are
absolute rights observable in the structure of the universe the way
that scotch tape or radioactivity are observable.

Perry

