At 10:37 AM 9/10/93 +0000, starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu wrote:
>Both concepts and rights are most certainly discoveries, not inventions.

Are these the only alternatives? How about emergent phenomena?

>Primarily, rights are not the result of human design, just like money,
>language, and many other social formations.  They result from human action,
>but not from human design.

But language, money, etc. are not found either. 

Okay, so I'm nit-picking.

At 10:28 AM 9/10/93 -0500, Freeman Craig Presson wrote:
>What justifies dropping money in with language and other
>culturally-evolved pragmas? 

This is well accepted among the Austrian Economists, as well as some other
schools of thought. Menger's version has money emerging slowly out of the
advantages of trading for something widely acceptable when traders can't
find someone with perfectly matched needs. I heard an alternative version
somewhere that began with money emerging from primitive accounting or debt
markers.

It's implausible that somebody just had the idea one day, 'Gee lets have a
common medium of exchange!' Human ingenuity was a necessary prerequisite
for money's emergence, but no one invented it.

