I guess I wasn't being clear. Of course the use of money _evolved_. I
had in mind to distinguish things like the use of money as being much
more abstract and requiring a higher degree of negotiation (literally
:-) than things like language; in fact, something like the use of
money could not even arise until language and custom prepared a place
for it. Language, like tool-making, can be seen to be rooted in
behavior we clearly class as instinctual, but the same cannot be said
for money.

As for whether there ever was a unique space-time event in which we
could say that the use of money was "invented" or "pioneered", we'll
never have the evidence; and the theories you cite above, while
appealing to common sense, can't be empirically supported. It would
seem very natural, in a culture where bands would meet periodically to
exchange members, stories, and crafts, that trade would lead to
lending/borrowing, and someone somewhere would have pioneered the IOU
in some form -- only when the number of people became large enough to
strain the memory, and it became necessary to have dealings with
people who were not close kin. You'd think anthropology would shed
some light on this, but while there are many anecdotes about money and
property in primitive cultures[1], there's no close enough correlation
among them to point to one origin.

