Date: Fri, 10 Sep 93 12:00:14 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Technological Infrastructure

How does this essay relate to Extropians? First, a couple of people
have asked about infrastructure needs for modern businesses. Second,
this relates to just how imminent is the phase change to telecommuting.

Mark Grant comments on my points (by the way, be careful to adjust
line length to less than 80 characters, to avoid the effect below):

> Hmm, with the possible aside of investment, I don't really see that there's 
> much a small high-tech company needs other than an Internet link (available
> anywhere on the planet if you're willing to pay for it), a nearby airport, and 
> reliable power and telephones. Lower wage rates aren't going to help when you're 

The "infrastructure" needed is largely underestimated by folks,
especially those thinking all one needs is a Net connection.

Let me briefly outline some of the things needed for a modern chip
factory, as an example. (I'll let Perry M. report, if he wishes, on
why a "pure information" business like his brokerage firm is located
in New York City and not Bozeman, Montana, or Martinique, or even New
Orleans.)

My background was in chips, at Intel. I worked in Silicon Valley
(before it was even dubbed that by a newsletter writer) and was then
transferred to the new "Memory Division" in scenic Aloha, Oregon,
about 15 miles west of Portland. The problems we had in even such a
relatively large metropolitan area are the basis of my comments.

A "software factory," such as Microsoft, Borland, or Novell, has differing
needs, but some of the factors are the same, especially those relating
to the pool of people.

Infrastructure things that are important:

* A pool of potential employees, unless all are to be relocated to a
remote area (as with chip plants in remote regions of Idaho, Utah,
etc.)--this is possible, but limits the option of both employer and
employees, who can feel "trapped" in a one-company town. This means a
certain kind of employee chooses to locate to these small towns, and
another (more entrepreneurial, usually) type chooses to stay in large,
diverse areas like the Silicon Valley.

* Quality of life is more than just open land and trees, as in
Bozeman, Pullyalup, whatever. Many engineers would rather live in the
Bay Area than in some remote location. The high housing price is the
*result* of many people wanting to live there! (An important point, no
doubt familiar to Extropians. If everybody wanted to leave the Bay
Area, prices on houses would drop precipitously.)

Also, the cultural attractions of the San Francisco area are important
for many (a fair number of engineers and software types live in San
Francisco and make the 40-mile drive down to the Palo Alto area every
day, or several days a week). Things like the "Assembler Multitudes"
nanotech discussion group, the Cypherpunks group, and the large number
of hackers and Extropians in the Bay Area are also a draw for many. 

* Universities. The role of Stanford is seminal in the Valley. Ditto
for Berkeley, and even the excellent state schools like San Jose
State. Santa Clara University, UC Santa Cruz, and lots of others
schools provide engineers and technicians. And many graduates of other
California schools (all UC campuses, Caltech, USC, etc.) know the Bay
Area quite well and will often take jobs there over jobs in, say,
Bozeman or Orem.

* A feeder system of equipment companies, machine shops, consultants,
etc. Not all things can be FedExed easily. In person visits are
needed. Factories are not self-contained entities, taking in only
shipments from other factories. The Silicon Valley has every major
equipment supplier (and multimillion dollar wafer steppers from Nikon
and Canon are best inspected at the vendor....it's real hard to
"FedEx" them to a remote site for inspection!), every major material
supplier (silicon, photoresist, masks, packaging, etc.). All this
stuff makes it very easy to do business in the Valley.

I could go on, but I think I've made the point.

Metropolitan areas like the Bay Area have some downsides, of course.
I, for example, live about 40 miles southwest of the Valley, across a
mountain range and on the coast. For me, being a man of leisure (Ha!),
the ocean and redwoods and generally less hectic lifestyle out here in
Santa Cruz is very attractive. I end up travelling to the Valley about
once a week or so.


> I keep hearing about this telecommuting age, and it never seems to happen. Maybe
> that's just because it's so damn expensive at the moment, but I find it difficult
> to see how I could do the work I do without 10 Mbps links to the other machines
> here, and without being able to just wander around and find the experts on
> different areas of our software products when I need them. What sort of jobs
> are people expecting telecommuters to do ? Or are we expecting everyone to have
> 10 Mbps links cheaper than telephone calls in the near future ?


Telecommuting has problems. Lack of face to face contact, difficulty
many people have in setting their own schedules and sticking to them,
complaints by other workers that "Joe is goofing off" at home, and so
on. The actual Net connection is not that big a deal (SLIP lines allow
unlimited Net access, phone calls are cheaper than hourly labor rates,
etc.).

Videoconferencing may help, but I don't think that's the main issue.

Better estimators for labor will help. "Piece work" helps, as with
freelance writers, software coders, etc.: they get paid by what they
actually _do_, not by how many hours they sit and stare at their
computer terminals.

I'll say that any startup company I may get involved with had better
be based on distributed, decentralized, telecommuting-based
principles. The problems that exist are solvable with enough effort
and enough dedication to the business goals by the partners/employees.

(There's a lot to say here, and I think this issue of telecommuting
would make a fine thread for Extropians to talk about, but not now.)

-Tim May


